tl;dr: With the caveat that the text that might go in 8446bis is more =
important, I think kitten should probably follow tls=E2=80=99 lead here =
and not include the header.

The updates header has been the source of numerous debates for over a =
decade. It has been used to indicate critical normative updates, =
optional extensions, and what I would call =E2=80=9Cbread crumbs=E2=80=9D =
to find related RFCs. In tls, we have probably used each of these [0]. =
But, the new normal is not including the updates header. RFC 8773, which =
is experimental, changes the behavior of TLS 1.3 to permit the server to =
send a CertificateRequest message when a PSK is being used and it did =
not get an updates header. draft-ietf-tls-subcerts is adding a mechanism =
to allow servers to use a delegated credential and it=E2=80=99s not got =
an updates header. EAP-TLS 1.3 doesn=E2=80=99t have an updates header.

I feel your pain about needing to get the word out, but I am not sure =
where it stops. If everybody wants a breadcrumb, the splash page for the =
RFC will be about 3 pages long as it references all of the RFCs that now =
refer to it and the I-Ds that do. Check out the lengthy list here:
https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/rfc8446/referencedby/

Finally, even with the =E2=80=9Cliberal" policy back when TLS 1.2 was =
newer, the original tls-unique didn=E2=80=99t get an updates header.

If you really want bread crumbs, what I would do is find all the places =
tls-unique is used =
(https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/rfc5929/referencedby/) in TLS 1.2 and =
make sure for the TLS 1.3 a reference to this I-D gets in. Sorry I just =
noticed this now, but top =E2=80=98o the list would be:
https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-ace-coap-est/
This I-D references possibly including an updated tls-unique mechanism =
for 1.3 (see s10.1) and is therefore ripe for a reference. I will note =
it is in the RFC editor=E2=80=99s queue awaiting publication but Ben can =
work magic.

spt

[0] The extreme case is probably where TLS 1.2 [RFC5246] is =E2=80=9Cupdat=
ed" by an experimental RFC from the ISE stream.

